% A builder plans a house in seven tasks, each taking a number of days and
% some waiting for others to finish. The critical path method works forward
% for the earliest day each task can start and backward for the latest day it
% must start without delaying the house. The house is finished on day 31;
% roofing has 9 days of slack and the electrics 1, and the foundation,
% framing, plumbing, drywall and painting are critical: any delay in them
% delays the house.
% Shows recursion through a maximum over the tasks before and a minimum over
% the tasks after, a task with none of either, and arithmetic on days.
% After the critical path method of Kelley and Walker (1959).

the templates are:
    *a task* takes *a number* days,
    *a task* comes after *a task*,
    *a task* can start on day *a number*,
    *a task* can finish on day *a number*,
    the house is finished on day *a number*,
    *a task* must finish by day *a number*,
    *a task* must start by day *a number*,
    *a task* has *a number* days of slack,
    *a task* is critical.

the knowledge base critical path includes:

% Forward: a task starts when the last task it comes after is finished.
a task can start on day 0
    if the task takes a number days
    and it is not the case that
        the task comes after an other task.

a task can start on day a number S
    if the task comes after an other task
    and S is the max of each F such that
        the task comes after a previous task
        and the previous task can finish on day F.

a task can finish on day a number F
    if the task can start on day a number S
    and the task takes a number D days
    and F = S + D.

the house is finished on day a number N
    if N is the max of each F such that
        a task can finish on day F.

% Backward: a task must finish before the first task after it must start.
a task must finish by day a number L
    if the task takes a number days
    and it is not the case that
        an other task comes after the task
    and the house is finished on day L.

a task must finish by day a number L
    if an other task comes after the task
    and L is the min of each X such that
        a next task comes after the task
        and the next task must start by day X.

a task must start by day a number X
    if the task must finish by day a number L
    and the task takes a number D days
    and X = L - D.

a task has a number K days of slack
    if the task can start on day a number S
    and the task must start by day a number X
    and K = X - S.

a task is critical
    if the task has 0 days of slack.

scenario house is:
    foundation takes 5 days.
    framing takes 10 days.
    roofing takes 4 days.
    plumbing takes 6 days.
    electrics takes 5 days.
    drywall takes 7 days.
    painting takes 3 days.
    framing comes after foundation.
    roofing comes after framing.
    plumbing comes after framing.
    electrics comes after framing.
    drywall comes after plumbing.
    drywall comes after electrics.
    painting comes after drywall.
    painting comes after roofing.
    finish expects answers ["the house is finished on day 31"].
    starts expects answers ["foundation can start on day 0", "framing can start on day 5",
        "roofing can start on day 15", "plumbing can start on day 15", "electrics can start on day 15",
        "drywall can start on day 21", "painting can start on day 28"].
    slack expects answers ["foundation has 0 days of slack", "framing has 0 days of slack",
        "roofing has 9 days of slack", "plumbing has 0 days of slack", "electrics has 1 days of slack",
        "drywall has 0 days of slack", "painting has 0 days of slack"].
    critical expects answers ["foundation is critical", "framing is critical", "plumbing is critical",
        "drywall is critical", "painting is critical"].

query finish is:
    the house is finished on day which number.

query starts is:
    which task can start on day which number.

query slack is:
    which task has which number days of slack.

query critical is:
    which task is critical.
